 ## Overview
-This method accelerates the ray marching of implicit surfaces defined by a sum of bounded functions $F_n(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i=1}^n f_i(\mathbf{p})$ by exploiting the known value ranges of individual terms [S1]. It constructs an implicit hierarchy where partial sums serve as bounding volumes for the final sum, allowing early termination when the potential range of the total function excludes the isosurface threshold [S1]. It differs from explicit BVH approaches by computing bounds on-the-fly from pre-sorted function ranges rather than traversing a spatial tree structure [S1].
+This method accelerates ray marching for implicit surfaces defined by a sum of bounded functions $F_n(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i=1}^n f_i(\mathbf{p})$ by exploiting known value ranges of individual terms [S1]. It constructs an implicit hierarchy where partial sums act as bounding volumes for the final sum, enabling early termination when the potential range of the total function excludes the isosurface threshold [S1]. It differs from explicit spatial BVH approaches by computing bounds on-the-fly from pre-sorted function ranges rather than traversing a tree structure [S1].
 
